\f3\u-10179?\u-8464?\u-497?\f1  \f0 Why GPS Needs Relativistic Corrections\par
GPS satellites orbit Earth at high speeds and altitudes, which means two key relativistic effects come into play:\par
Special Relativity (SR):\par
Because the satellites are moving quickly relative to observers on Earth, their onboard atomic clocks tick slightly slower than Earth-based clocks.\par
This is time dilation due to velocity.\par
General Relativity (GR):\par
The satellites are farther from Earth\f1\rquote\f0 s gravitational field, so they experience less gravitational time dilation.\par
Their clocks tick faster than those on Earth.\par
Combined, these effects cause GPS satellite clocks to run about 38 microseconds faster per day than Earth clocks. If left uncorrected, this would result in positioning errors of several kilometers per day.\par
